# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Turn the elaborated design into a gate netlist Icarus can simulate.
#
# The caller has already read the RTL and run `hierarchy` with the geometry and
# MAX_M it wants, so this file must not do either -- it would undo the -chparam
# overrides scripts/gatelevel.sh just applied. The caller also owns the
# write_verilog, because only it knows where the netlist should land.
#
# This is Yosys's generic synthesis, not Quartus's or Vivado's. What it proves is
# that the RTL means the same thing after *a* synthesiser has had its way with
# it, and that the result comes up cleanly from reset. It is not a substitute for
# the vendor builds, and it is not a claim about either vendor's optimiser.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# -flatten because the point is to simulate gates. Module boundaries surviving
# into the netlist would leave whole subtrees un-optimised and un-mapped, which
# is the part of the transformation most worth checking.
synth -top systolic_tile -flatten

# The netlist would otherwise be a second module named systolic_tile, clashing
# with gl/gl_shim.sv -- which has to keep that name, because it is the name
# tb_systolic_tile instantiates.
rename systolic_tile systolic_tile_gates
